  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 249 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nNorthwest flow aloft on Sunday will gradually transition to more \nzonal flow through Tuesday night. Flow aloft will then become \nincreasingly southwesterly on Wednesday ahead of a potent shortwave \ntrough forecast to close off near the Four Corners region by \nWednesday night. This system could potentially impact the forecast \narea on Thursday. Given this is several days out, differences among \nthe deterministic guidance are not unexpected. The latest GFS run \ndepicts a stronger northern stream, resulting in a more suppressed \nstorm track that keeps the system primarily south of the forecast \narea. Meanwhile, the operational ECMWF is weaker with the northern \nstream, allowing for a farther north track that would bring \npotential impacts to western and north central Nebraska.\n\nFrom an ensemble perspective, both the ENS and GEFS are in \nrelatively good agreement, with probabilities of at least 40 percent \nfor a tenth of an inch of liquid precipitation extending roughly \nfrom an O'Neill to Thedford to Sidney line and points southward. \nThis system will need to be closely monitored over the next several \nmodel cycles as the evolution and eventual track become better \ndefined. An open Gulf ahead of the system appears likely, supporting \na decent northward moisture flux into the southern Plains before \nwrapping back westward to the north and west of the surface low. One \nmissing ingredient at this point appears to be a lack of \nsufficiently cold air, as there is no apparent connection to a \ncolder Canadian airmass, which looks to remain bottled up to our \nnorth in association with the northern stream. However, this could \nchange as the system evolves, and both thermal profiles and \npotential precipitation types will need to be monitored in coming \nmodel runs.\n\nPrior to Thursday's system, a warming trend is expected, with below-\nnormal temperatures Sunday transitioning to above-normal temperatures\nMonday through Wednesday. Tuesday currently looks to be the warmest\nday, with highs in the upper 50s to near 60 degrees across portions\nof southwest Nebraska south of Interstate 80. Cooler temperatures\nare then expected Thursday as the potential storm system approaches\nthe region.",
